= Related Work <sec:related>

*Enforcement makes a cartel, and it is tied to the partner.* Without a binding contract, collusion survives only if a defection is expected to be punished @friedman1971 @greenporter1984. Q-learning pricing algorithms learn such punishments on their own: forced to cut for one period, they punish for a few rounds and return to the high price @calvano2020. On the path of play, LLM pricing agents answer a rival's cuts in the same way @fish2024. But a learned punishment answers a particular rival: collusive Q-learning policies fall to Nash play on average against an unseen rival @eschenbaum2022. Same-family models coordinate better than mixed ones in pure coordination games @aharon2026, which motivates a same-model successor as the likeliest case. To our knowledge, no study has replaced a member of a working LLM cartel and asked whether its punishment survives.

*An LLM agent's strategy lives in its context.* Each round a fixed model reads its prompt, the price history and its own notes, and the notes matter. In #cite(<fish2024>, form: "prose"), agents whose notes were erased every round reached the monopoly price in 6 of 12 runs, against 12 of 12 with them, and planting one sentence against price wars in a seller's plans raised its next price. Our notes arms implant a predecessor's full notes, after 150 rounds of cartel, in a new seller.

*High prices without enforcement.* Q-learning agents that keep the same partner raise prices even when a cut cannot be seen or punished @arslan2026. Proposed liability tests and audits look instead for reward and punishment in a firm's frozen pricing policy @harrington2018 @eschenbaum2026. Part of an LLM seller's policy is not frozen: it sits in notes that a successor can inherit. But notes do not reveal intent: chain-of-thought faithfulness and collusion dissociate in LLM pricing agents @leepark2026, and some newcomers here cite the inherited rule without acting on it (@sec:notes). So we vary the notes as an input and test enforcement by a forced cut.

*What our design adds.* Unlike prior work, which changes the environment or edits notes in place @arslan2026 @fish2024, we replace the seller and vary what its successor inherits. Either inheritance keeps the cartel price, but only the notes carry enforcement (@sec:results), which is the distinction a rule on inherited data would need (@sec:intro).
